From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 00:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] py/scheduler: Don't run callbacks between disable_irq() and
 enable_irq().

The VM checks for pending callbacks at every backward jump, so a loop
between machine.disable_irq() and machine.enable_irq() runs any callback
that is due there, with interrupts still off. usb.device's Buffer does
this: finish_read() and finish_write() shuffle bytes in a loop inside
disable_irq(), and they run for every USB transfer.

On esp32, disable_irq() is a FreeRTOS critical section. A callback that
waits on an interrupt then waits inside it: an I2C transfer blocks on its
completion interrupt, the yield that would switch away can't fire, and the
task places itself on the I2C queue's wait list a second time. The list
links to itself and the next insert spins, until the interrupt watchdog
resets the chip ("Interrupt wdt timeout on CPU1", in vListInsert under
xQueueReceive in the I2C master driver). An LVGL app polling a touch panel
from a timer while a USB MIDI device sends hit it within a minute; a
3 ms timer reading an I2C register while the USB device echoes MIDI hits
it in one to four seconds.

Leave callbacks pending while the atomic section is nested. They run at
the next check after enable_irq(). Ports opt in by defining
MICROPY_ATOMIC_SECTION_NESTED(); esp32 reads the depth of its recursive
mp_atomic_mux.

diff --git a/ports/esp32/mphalport.h b/ports/esp32/mphalport.h
--- a/ports/esp32/mphalport.h
+++ b/ports/esp32/mphalport.h
@@ -90,6 +90,10 @@
 #define MICROPY_BEGIN_ATOMIC_SECTION() mp_begin_atomic_section()
 #define MICROPY_END_ATOMIC_SECTION(state) mp_end_atomic_section(state)
 
+// Whether the atomic section just taken is nested inside another on this core.
+// mp_atomic_mux is a recursive spinlock and its count is the nesting depth.
+#define MICROPY_ATOMIC_SECTION_NESTED() (mp_atomic_mux.count > 1)
+
 __attribute__((always_inline)) static inline mp_uint_t mp_hal_ticks_us(void) {
     return esp_timer_get_time();
 }
diff --git a/py/scheduler.c b/py/scheduler.c
--- a/py/scheduler.c
+++ b/py/scheduler.c
@@ -82,6 +82,17 @@
         return;
     }
 
+    #ifdef MICROPY_ATOMIC_SECTION_NESTED
+    if (MICROPY_ATOMIC_SECTION_NESTED()) {
+        // We were called from inside an atomic section, such as Python code
+        // between machine.disable_irq() and machine.enable_irq(). A callback
+        // that waits on an interrupt (an I2C transfer, a sleep) would wait with
+        // interrupts off, so leave the callbacks pending until it ends.
+        MICROPY_END_ATOMIC_SECTION(atomic_state);
+        return;
+    }
+    #endif
+
     // Equivalent to mp_sched_lock(), but we're already in the atomic
     // section and know that we're pending.
     MP_STATE_VM(sched_state) = MP_SCHED_LOCKED;
